<SiteLayout title="Variables and method">
  <h1>A task-fit estimate is <span style="color:var(--cyan)">an engineering argument.</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">Conventional software engineers budget work, memory and worst-case timing against a specified behavior. For a learned system we first ask a limit question: could any model within a parameter, information and compute budget reach the required task performance? We then ask whether a particular trained release approaches that limit.</p>
  <div class="note"><strong>Interpretation:</strong> The existing calculator is an explicit, uncalibrated hypothesis. Its ordinary/tail coverage curves and assembly proxies are not observed scaling laws. The benchmark atlas gives attained examples, which bound the unknown best-achievable frontier from below. Absence of a successful smaller model is not an impossibility proof.</div>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Ceiling, floor, and attained release</div><h2>The frontier we actually want</h2><p>For a fixed task, operating domain, sensor representation and available training evidence, define <code>F*(P,C)</code> as the supremum task fitness attainable by any allowed frozen model using <em>at most</em> P parameters and deployment compute budget C. Under that definition, enlarging P cannot lower the ceiling because the smaller models remain allowed. It may plateau, and changing the task or representation changes the whole curve.</p><p>A deployed model has an attained score below or at that unknown ceiling. Its lower-tail performance across named edge-case slices can lag further. Training, data curation, architecture and optimization determine the gap. Raising parameter capacity only helps if these other parts exploit it.</p><p>The engineering question is therefore to find <code>Pareto-minimal (P,C) such that F*ordinary ≥ targetordinary and F*tail ≥ targettail</code>, subject to observation and timing limits. Current public benchmark points supply attained <em>lower</em> bounds on F*. To rule a budget out requires a genuine upper bound, a proven toy restriction, or an explicitly uncertain extrapolation from well-calibrated model sweeps.</p></section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Exact risk ledger / under declared task and loss</div><h2>Where the limit comes from</h2><p>Let <code>Rraw</code> be the best possible risk with raw observations, <code>Rrepr</code> the best possible risk after the chosen sensor/token representation, <code>Rbudget</code> the best possible risk among models meeting the P and C budgets, and <code>Rrelease</code> the risk of the actual frozen release. For nested allowed sets and the same loss/distribution:</p><p class="bigreadout"><code>Rrelease = Rraw + (Rrepr − Rraw) + (Rbudget − Rrepr) + (Rrelease − Rbudget)</code></p><p>The terms are, in order: irreducible task uncertainty, information lost by the representation, the capacity/compute gap, and the gap left by training and implementation. This is an exact telescoping identity from the definitions; it does not assume squared error. The middle quantities are usually unknown, so the equation organizes what needs bounding. Hardware timing constrains which models enter <code>Rbudget</code>; it is not another additive accuracy penalty.</p><p class="source">A special-case theoretical example is <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01145" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yarotsky's approximation lower bound</a> for an entire Sobolev function class with continuous weight selection. Another is <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/safran17a.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Safran and Shamir's depth/width separation</a> for a ball indicator under specified distributions. Neither gives a model-size minimum for driving or DAA.</p></section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Working metatheory / a falsifiable decomposition</div><h2>Four gates before a model can be fit for a task</h2><div class="grid"><div><h3>1. Observation</h3><p>Can the sensors and representation preserve the distinction that the correct action depends on? This includes optics, range, resolution, temporal history, occlusion and uncertainty. If relevant states look identical to the system, more weights cannot recover the lost information.</p></div><div><h3>2. Learnability</h3><p>Does the training distribution expose the necessary rules and combinations? Task-conditioned reusable structure might lower the description cost; rare or novel cases raise the data and evidence burden. This is the proposed place for assembly ideas.</p></div><div><h3>3. Model capacity</h3><p>Within a fixed model family, search across training recipes and data to estimate the best-achievable score at each parameter budget. The upper envelope of observed scores is a lower bound on the true capacity ceiling. Fit an extrapolation with uncertainty to estimate a possible minimum for a specified benchmark slice.</p></div><div><h3>4. Deployment</h3><p>Can the frozen model and entire pipeline meet memory, bandwidth, power and deadline requirements on the intended hardware, including concurrent sensors and rare scheduling spikes? A compute roofline is only a necessary screen.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Proposed equations / status made explicit</div><h2>What can be bounded, and what must be fitted?</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Use</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>T = Σᵢ Fᵢ⌈Wᵢ/pᵢ⌉⌈Hᵢ/pᵢ⌉</code></td><td>Visual token load for patchified streams; compare with context X.</td><td>Exact count under the stated patching rule, not an information sufficiency test.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>eₛ(P,n) ≈ e₀,ₛ + Aₛ(P/P₀)^−αₛ + Bₛ(nₛ/n₀)^−βₛ</code></td><td>Candidate fit for the error of the best attained model in held-out slice s. Exponents and irreducible floor must be measured; fit only over a bounded range.</td><td>Hypothesis, not a universal law. Slice errors are different from safety-event rates.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Gₛ = task-conditional grammar cost + exception cost</code></td><td>Proposed assembly descriptor under a fixed primitive vocabulary. Test whether Gₛ predicts fitted curve parameters beyond ordinary compression and pilot-only baselines.</td><td>Research hypothesis. Raw image assembly alone fails the same-images/different-label-rule counterexample.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>M ≥ P·b/8 + M<sub>activations</sub> + M<sub>state</sub> + M<sub>runtime</sub></code></td><td>Necessary deployment memory accounting.</td><td>Engineering identity once each term is measured. The current calculator only approximates weights and KV.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>t ≥ max(F/C<sub>eff</sub>, Q/B<sub>eff</sub>) + t<sub>fixed</sub></code></td><td>Optimistic roofline for work F, traffic Q, effective arithmetic C and bandwidth B.</td><td>Lower-bound screen; scheduling, stalls and tail latency require profiling.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Σₖ rₖFₖ ≤ C<sub>eff</sub></code></td><td>Aggregate rate condition for concurrent camera and model workloads.</td><td>Necessary throughput screen, never sufficient for per-decision latency.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="source">Research ingredients: <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06600-9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">assembly theory</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.13506" target="_blank" rel="noopener">skill-frequency scaling</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.10802" target="_blank" rel="noopener">manifold-dependent scaling</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556" target="_blank" rel="noopener">compute/data scaling</a>, and <a href="https://www2.eecs.berkeley.edu/Pubs/TechRpts/2008/EECS-2008-134.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">roofline analysis</a>. None of these sources validates the combined Gₛ hypothesis.</p>
  </section>

