  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Research design / falsifiers</div><h2>How the metatheory can earn predictive value</h2><ol><li>Define one task and its operating-domain slices before choosing a model family: ordinary, tail, range, weather, target class and latency.</li><li>Measure the observation floor with controlled sensor/crop/temporal ablations. Compare physically different states that the representation maps together.</li><li>Train a matrix of model sizes and independent data budgets with the same architecture family and fixed evaluation protocol. Record seeds and training recipes.</li><li>At each budget, run multiple architectures, seeds and training recipes; record the best attained score and its lower-tail slice results. Fit a frontier on some sizes, then predict held-out larger budgets and domain shifts. Compare task-conditional grammar descriptors with raw compression, simple dataset statistics and pilot-only fits.</li><li>Benchmark the exact frozen pipeline on target hardware for throughput, memory, p99/p99.9 latency and thermal steady state. Leave explicit margin for action timing.</li><li>Challenge the estimator with counterexamples: same pixels but changed labels, same parameter count but different training data, coarse tokens retaining a small cue, and a model that meets average FPS but misses deadlines.</li></ol><p><strong>Success criterion:</strong> an estimator should predict held-out task-slice performance and necessary resource ranges more accurately than simpler baselines, while identifying when it has insufficient data to predict. Even a successful estimator would support a bounded fit argument, not certify system safety.</p></section>

  <section class="card"><h2>Limits of the current atlas</h2><p>The 139 TorchVision points are attained weight configurations from one library release, not upper bounds on what each parameter count could possibly do. Several configurations share architectures, training data or recipes. The 46 AVOIDDS slice observations come from two models tested on overlapping subsets. They are valuable contrasts, but they are not 185 independent task families. We do not fit a universal regression across the four TorchVision metrics, LFW accuracy, DAA mAP and flight range.</p><p>For video discussion, the strongest candidate pairs are ResNet-50 V1/V2 (same size and work, different attainment), AVOIDDS full/restricted training (same architecture, different attainment and simulated outcome), NASA small UAS/SR22 (same camera, different visibility), and Tesla AI4/AI5 (public roadmap ratios with an unknown absolute baseline).</p></section>
